
Since your report reads from tbl_participants and only has CardSerialNo, you need to resolve that card to the employee using both RFID rules:

Old RFID → vw_UserMaster_CardSerialNo.CardSerialNo

New RFID → TBL_M_USERMASTER.EM (first 10 characters sometimes)


You can join both conditions when linking to TBL_M_USERMASTER.

Recommended Reporting Query

SELECT 
    h.EmployeeNo,
    CONCAT(h.FirstName, ' ', COALESCE(h.MiddleName, ''), ' ', h.LastName) AS FullName,
    h.Grade,
    CONCAT(h.DivisionName, '/', h.DepartmentName, '/', h.SectionName) AS Department,
    m.TapIn,
    m.TapOut
FROM tbl_participants m
LEFT JOIN dbo.TBL_M_USERMASTER h
    ON 
        -- New RFID match
        (CASE 
            WHEN LEN(h.EM) > 10 THEN SUBSTRING(h.EM,1,10)
            ELSE h.EM
        END = m.CardSerialNo)

        OR

        -- Old RFID match
        EXISTS (
            SELECT 1
            FROM dbo.vw_UserMaster_CardSerialNo v
            WHERE v.APOAccount = h.APOAccount
            AND v.CardSerialNo = m.CardSerialNo
        )

WHERE m.MeetingId = @meetingid
ORDER BY m.id DESC;

How it Works

When generating the report:

1. tbl_participants.CardSerialNo is checked against new RFID (EM).


2. If not matched, it checks old RFID (vw_UserMaster_CardSerialNo).


3. If either matches, it retrieves the employee info.



So your report supports both IDs used during tapping.

Data Flow

tbl_participants.CardSerialNo
        │
        ├─ New RFID → TBL_M_USERMASTER.EM
        │
        └─ Old RFID → vw_UserMaster_CardSerialNo → APOAccount → TBL_M_USERMASTER

Benefit

✔ Works for both old and new ID cards
✔ Uses only tbl_participants.CardSerialNo (good for reports)
✔ No temp table or union needed


